Cardiologent at the Patient Level


The patient level assembles the window verdicts, with the patient’s metadata and
retrieved guidelines, into the three-part assessment; the baselines are asked the
same three questions on the same record. Cardiologent leads on all three
(Table 4), by the widest margin on P1, the integrated diagnosis
the assembly is built to reach. Two cardiologists blind to system identity rank
the systems in the judge’s order and place Cardiologent first throughout;
their agreement with the judge (ICC 0.740.74, 0.660.66) matches their agreement with
each other (0.670.67, Figure 4), so the at-scale judge stands in for a
clinician about as well as one clinician stands in for another.


We also score the diagnosis directly—the dominant rhythm named exactly, within
its management family, or anywhere at all (supplement)—where
Cardiologent leads at every grade and the baselines exceed a third only
at the loosest.


Table 4: Patient-level assessment on the three tasks, each scored on two
axes (SUM/8/8). Top: the LLM judge (DeepSeek-V4-Pro) over all 6565 test patients;
bottom: two cardiologists, blind to system identity, over a 2020-patient subset
drawn at random but stratified to preserve the arrhythmia-class proportions of
the full test set (per-class counts in the supplement).
